<h1>Conveyor System Design Guide</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor system design is the engineering process of selecting, sizing, and laying out conveyors to move products through a packaging line at a target throughput rate. The process starts with defining product characteristics, throughput requirements, and environmental conditions, then proceeds through conveyor type selection, layout planning, controls architecture, and integration with upstream and downstream packaging machines. This guide covers every phase of conveyor system design and links to detailed sub-guides on <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/components/">conveyor frames, drives, and pulleys</a> and <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">conveyor engineering calculations</a>.</p>
<h2 id="requirements-heading">Requirements Gathering</h2>
<p>Every conveyor design project begins with a detailed requirements gathering phase. The information collected here determines every downstream decision, conveyor type, frame material, belt material, motor size, controls complexity, and cost. Skipping or rushing this phase is a common cause of conveyor system redesigns and change orders.</p>
<h3>Product Characteristics</h3>
<p>Document every product that will run on the conveyor system. Record the minimum, maximum, and typical values for length, width, height, and weight. Irregular shapes, flexible packaging, and round products (bottles, cans, jars) each require different conveyor surfaces and guide rail configurations. Note product surface characteristics, smooth, rough, sticky, oily, or dusty, because surface friction affects <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">belt material selection</a> and incline angle limits.</p>
<p>Fragile products require low-impact transfers and accumulation zones that prevent product-to-product contact pressure. Temperature-sensitive products (chocolate, frozen goods, pharmaceuticals) dictate environmental controls and belt material temperature ratings. Record the product packaging type: bare product, pouches, cartons, cases, shrink-wrapped bundles, or pallets. Each packaging format has different conveying requirements.</p>
<h3>Throughput Requirements</h3>
<p>Define throughput in the units that matter to production: products per minute, cases per hour, pallets per shift, or pounds per hour. Conveyor speed and width are calculated directly from throughput. A single-file conveyor running at 60 feet per minute with a 12-inch product pitch delivers 60 products per minute. This is arithmetic: products per minute equals belt speed divided by product pitch, in matching units. Higher throughput requires wider conveyors for multi-lane operation, faster belt speeds, or both.</p>
<p>Account for peak throughput, not just average. Upstream machines often surge above their average rate, for example while catching up after a short stop. The conveyor system must handle peak rates without jamming. Size conveyors to the maximum rated speed of the upstream machine plus a stated margin, and write that margin into the specification so every bidder designs to the same number.</p>
<h3>Environmental Conditions</h3>
<p>Environment determines frame material, belt material, motor enclosure rating, and overall system cost. Standard dry environments use painted carbon steel frames and PVC belts at the lowest cost point. Washdown environments (USDA, dairy, meat, bakery) require stainless steel frames, polyurethane or modular plastic belts, and sealed motors with washdown-rated enclosures, typically specified by IP rating (for example IP69K). For belt material details, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">conveyor belting guide</a>.</p>
<p>Cleanroom environments (pharmaceutical, medical device) require smooth, non-particle-shedding surfaces, enclosed drives, and HEPA-compatible designs. Hazardous locations (Class I or II, Division 1 or 2 under the National Electrical Code) require motors and controls listed for that classification. Cold storage and freezer environments require components rated for the room's lowest temperature, condensation management, and low-temperature lubricants.</p>
<h2 id="layout-design">Conveyor Layout Design</h2>
<p>To lay out a packaging line conveyor system, place the machines first, then connect them with conveyors in this order: rates, buffers, transfers and turns, elevation, access and guarding, and control zones. Each step constrains the next, so a layout drawn in a different order usually gets redrawn.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Map machines and rates.</strong> Draw every machine at scale with its infeed and discharge height, product orientation, and rated speed. Mark the slowest machine. It sets line output, and the conveyors around it should be sized to keep it fed.</li>
<li><strong>Place accumulation between machines with different uptimes.</strong> Machines stop at different times for jams, reel changes, and adjustments. Put <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">buffer conveyors</a> ahead of the critical machine and between machines that stop independently. Size each buffer from the stop length you want to ride through multiplied by the upstream rate.</li>
<li><strong>Lay out transfers and curves.</strong> Choose the transfer type at each conveyor-to-conveyor and conveyor-to-machine joint before fixing conveyor lengths. Use <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyors</a> for right-angle moves and <a href="/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/">curved conveyors</a> where product must keep its orientation through a turn. Check that the smallest product can cross every gap.</li>
<li><strong>Add elevation changes.</strong> Route <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline conveyors</a>, spirals, or lifts where the line must rise to a machine infeed, a mezzanine, or over an aisle. Inclines take floor length, so place them before final positions are set.</li>
<li><strong>Set access and guarding.</strong> Leave maintenance access on both sides, operator stations at working height, crossovers or gates where people must cross the line, and guarding at every nip point and drive. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-safety/">conveyor safety guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Zone the controls.</strong> Divide the line into control zones at each machine, buffer, and merge or divert. Mark photoeye positions, E-stop locations, and which machine signals start and stop each zone.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Layout Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>No accumulation ahead of the slowest or least reliable machine, so every short stop there stops the line.</li>
<li>Conveyor lengths fixed before transfer types are chosen, leaving gaps the smallest product cannot cross.</li>
<li>Inclines added late, which forces steeper angles than the product can handle.</li>
<li>Machine infeed and discharge heights not confirmed, so transfers need last-minute height adjustments.</li>
<li>No crossover or gate where operators need to reach the other side of the line.</li>
<li>One long control zone, so a jam anywhere stops conveyors that could keep running.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="layout-heading">Layout Planning</h2>
<p>Layout planning translates throughput requirements and floor space constraints into a physical conveyor arrangement. Start with a scaled floor plan showing all existing equipment, columns, doorways, utilities, and operator access areas. Mark the product flow path from the start of the packaging line (infeed) to the end (palletizer or shipping).</p>
<p>Conveyor layouts follow one of four basic patterns: straight-line, L-shaped, U-shaped, or S-shaped. Straight-line layouts are simplest and lowest cost but require the most floor space in one direction. U-shaped layouts return the product to the same end of the building as the infeed, reducing forklift travel distance. L-shaped layouts fit into corner spaces. S-shaped layouts fit long processes into compact footprints.</p>
<h3>Elevation Changes</h3>
<p>Packaging lines frequently require elevation changes, from floor level to machine infeed height, between mezzanine levels, or over aisle crossings. <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">Incline conveyors</a> handle gradual elevation changes. The maximum angle depends on the product, its center of gravity, and the belt surface, so confirm it with the conveyor builder. Steeper angles require cleated belts, bucket elevators, or vertical reciprocating conveyors. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/">engineering calculations guide</a> covers incline angle limits for different product types.</p>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/">Spiral conveyors</a> provide continuous elevation change in a compact footprint. <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">Vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs)</a> handle pallets and heavy loads between levels. <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">Bucket elevators</a> handle bulk materials vertically.</p>
<h3>Transfer Points</h3>
<p>Transfer points, where product moves from one conveyor to another, or between a conveyor and a machine, are the most failure-prone locations in any conveyor system. Dead plates, nose bar transfers, and pop-up transfers each suit different product types. Small products and flexible packages require dead plate transfers with minimal gaps. Cases and totes transfer reliably between roller conveyors with powered transfer sections.</p>
<p>Every transfer point introduces a potential jam location. Minimize the number of transfers in the layout. When transfers are unavoidable, add photoeye sensors upstream and downstream of each transfer to detect jams early and stop upstream conveyors before product piles up. For a deeper look at transfer conveyor options, see the <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor types overview</a>.</p>
<h3>Operator Access and Ergonomics</h3>
<p>Design conveyor height for the operators and machines it serves: standing pack stations, seated inspection, and machine infeed heights often conflict, so set each height deliberately. Leave clear working space at manual pack stations and maintenance access on both sides of each conveyor. Where forklifts or other powered handling equipment operate, <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.176" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.176(a)</a> requires sufficient safe clearances for aisles, loading docks, doorways, and turns. Set specific clearances with your safety team and local code.</p>
<p>Place E-stop buttons within reach of every operator position. <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, the conveyor safety standard, covers emergency stop and other safety requirements for conveyor design, installation, and operation. Pull-cord E-stops provide continuous access along the full length of long conveyors. For full safety requirements, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-safety/">conveyor safety guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Conveyor Selection</h2>
<p>Conveyor type selection matches the product, function, and environment to the right conveyor technology. Each conveyor type excels at a specific set of tasks and fails at others. Choosing the wrong type creates chronic problems, jams, product damage, premature wear, and downtime, that no amount of adjustment can fix.</p>
<h3>Selection by Product Type</h3>
<p>Bottles, jars, and cylindrical containers run best on tabletop chain conveyors with side guide rails. The smooth, low-friction chain surface prevents bottles from tipping. Cartons, cases, and trays run well on both belt conveyors and roller conveyors. Belt conveyors provide positive control of product position; roller conveyors allow easy manual push-on and push-off at operator stations. Pouches, bags, and flexible packages require belt conveyors, roller conveyors allow flexible products to sag between rollers and jam.</p>
<h3>Selection by Function</h3>
<p>Transport conveyors move product from point A to point B at a constant speed. Belt conveyors and powered roller conveyors handle this function. Accumulation conveyors buffer product between machines running at different speeds. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/zero-pressure/">Zero-pressure accumulation conveyors</a> (MDR or zero-pressure roller) prevent product contact during accumulation, protecting fragile or labeled products. For more on accumulation, see the <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a> page.</p>
<p>Indexing conveyors start, stop, and position product at precise locations for filling, labeling, printing, or inspection. Servo-driven belt conveyors provide the position accuracy required for indexing. Merging and diverting conveyors combine or split product flows using pop-up transfers, pusher diverters, or <a href="/conveyor-types/activated-roller-belt/">activated roller belt (ARB)</a> technology.</p>
<h3>Selection by Environment</h3>
<p>Standard dry environments support any conveyor type. Washdown environments narrow the field to stainless steel frame conveyors with open-frame designs that shed water and allow cleaning access. Sanitary environments add requirements for crevice-free construction, tool-free belt removal, and FDA-compliant contact surfaces. Cold storage environments require components rated for sub-zero temperatures and designs that manage condensation.</p>
<p>Understanding the cost implications of each environment is important for budgeting. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> compares relative costs across environment types and conveyor categories.</p>
<h2 id="controls-heading">Controls Architecture</h2>
<p>Controls architecture defines how conveyors communicate with each other and with packaging machines. The complexity ranges from standalone conveyors with a simple on/off switch to fully networked systems with PLC control, HMI displays, and Ethernet/IP communication to every device on the line.</p>
<h3>Standalone Controls</h3>
<p>Standalone conveyors operate independently with a motor starter or VFD mounted on the conveyor frame. An operator uses a local start/stop station or speed dial to control the conveyor. Standalone controls work for simple transport conveyors that run continuously at a fixed speed. Cost is lowest because no PLC, no network wiring, and no programming are required.</p>
<h3>Distributed Controls</h3>
<p>Distributed controls place intelligence at each conveyor zone. MDR (motorized drive roller) conveyors use this approach, each zone has its own motor, controller card, and photoeye sensor. Zones communicate with neighboring zones using simple signal wires. When a downstream zone is full, the upstream zone stops. This provides zero-pressure accumulation without a central PLC. Distributed controls suit warehouse and distribution conveyor systems with long runs and many accumulation zones.</p>
<h3>Centralized PLC Controls</h3>
<p>Centralized PLC controls provide the highest level of coordination between conveyors and packaging machines. A programmable logic controller (PLC), typically Allen-Bradley (Rockwell Automation), Siemens, or Beckhoff, runs the control logic for all conveyors and communicates with upstream and downstream machines via Ethernet/IP, PROFINET, or EtherCAT. An HMI (human-machine interface) touchscreen provides operator control, speed adjustment, diagnostics, and alarm history.</p>
<p>Centralized controls are required for lines that perform indexing, product tracking, sortation, recipe-based changeover, or data logging. The PLC knows the position and speed of every conveyor, can synchronize conveyor speed to machine speed, and can implement complex routing logic for sortation and diverting. <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">Variable frequency drives</a> (VFDs) on each motor allow the PLC to adjust conveyor speed in real time. For motor and drive selection, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">motors and drives guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="integration-heading">Machine Integration</h2>
<p>Conveyors connect packaging machines into a production line. The integration between conveyors and machines determines line efficiency. Poor integration creates bottlenecks, jams, and product damage at every machine-to-conveyor transfer point.</p>
<p>Each packaging machine has a discharge height, discharge speed, and product orientation that the receiving conveyor must match. A case erector discharges flat cases at a specific height and speed, the receiving conveyor must match that height exactly and run at a speed that clears each case before the next one arrives. A carton closer or shrink wrapper discharges product in a specific orientation, the conveyor must maintain that orientation through any downstream turns or transfers.</p>
<p>Accumulation between machines is critical for line efficiency. Without accumulation, a jam or stoppage at any single machine stops the entire line. Add accumulation conveyors upstream of every machine that runs slower than the preceding machine. Size the accumulation zone from the length of the stops you want to ride through: buffer time multiplied by the upstream machine's rate gives the number of products to hold. This allows operators time to clear a jam or perform a short adjustment without stopping the upstream machines.</p>
<p>Speed matching between conveyors and machines prevents product gaps and bunching. When a conveyor feeds a machine, the conveyor speed should match or slightly exceed the machine's infeed speed. Running the conveyor too fast creates product collisions at the machine infeed. Running too slow starves the machine and reduces throughput. For high-speed lines, servo-driven indexing conveyors synchronize electronically to the machine cycle via encoder feedback.</p>
<p>The <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">conveyor manufacturers directory</a> lists companies that provide integrated conveyor and packaging line design services, from concept through commissioning.</p>
<h2 id="installation-heading">Installation Planning</h2>
<p>Installation planning begins during design, not after fabrication. Floor condition, power availability, compressed air supply, and rigging access all affect conveyor design decisions. Verify floor flatness against the conveyor builder's installation tolerance, because an uneven floor twists the frame and causes belt tracking and roller problems. Uneven floors require adjustable leg levelers on every support.</p>
<p>Power requirements depend on the total connected horsepower of all conveyor motors. Get the connected load from the conveyor builder's electrical drawings and confirm that the facility electrical panel has capacity for the additional load. Plan conduit routing from the electrical panel to each conveyor motor and control panel. Overhead cable tray is preferred over floor-level conduit to keep the floor clear for cleaning and forklift access.</p>
<p>Conveyor sections must fit through doorways, elevators, and aisles to reach the installation area. Section length is limited by truck size, door openings, and turning radius in hallways, so give the builder those dimensions. Longer conveyor runs are shipped in sections and bolted together on-site. Confirm rigging access, some installations require a crane or forklift to position elevated conveyors.</p>
<p>Factory acceptance testing (FAT) at the manufacturer's facility reduces installation time and risk. During FAT, the manufacturer assembles the conveyor system, runs test products, verifies speeds and throughput, and demonstrates controls functionality. Issues found during FAT are faster and cheaper to fix than issues found after shipping and installation. Site acceptance testing (SAT) after installation confirms that the system meets performance specifications in the production environment.</p>
